@*
    The chrome of the public surface, by the csx-arena design system.

    Three things in here are load-bearing and none of them are decoration.

    1. THE HEADER CARRIES BOTH MARKS. When the data was collected, and when this HTML was built.
       They are different facts (blueprint §5): a page served from a second-old cache was rendered
       now and collected then, and a reader who is given one number cannot tell which one it is.
       A page with nothing collected prints a dash there, never a zero and never the render time
       standing in for it.

    2. NOTHING IS FETCHED FROM ANYWHERE ELSE. No CDN, no analytics, no font host, no map tile.
       Every byte this page pulls comes from this application. A surface whose whole argument is
       "we do not show you anything we cannot account for" must not open the visitor's browser to
       a third party before it has said one true thing about a market.

    3. THE PAGE IS COMPLETE WITH SCRIPTS OFF. Every fade, every verdict, every age is in the HTML
       as delivered. The scripts below advance the ages and draw the candles; neither of them is
       what makes the page correct.
*@
@{
    var eyebrow = ViewData["Eyebrow"] as string;

    // The two header marks moved into _Stamps.cshtml, which reads CollectedFrom / CollectedTo / RenderedAt /
    // ShowCollected out of ViewData itself; only the render instant is still needed here, for the
    // clock anchor below.
    var renderedAt = ViewData["RenderedAt"] as DateTimeOffset?;

    // The anchor the client corrects its own clock against. It goes out as an absolute instant and
    // the script pairs it with performance.now(); see arena-ages.js. Without it a visitor whose
    // clock is forty seconds fast reads the entire page as degraded — every figure faded to the
    // floor, every age wearing △ — on data that is two seconds old.
    var serverNowMs = (renderedAt ?? DateTimeOffset.UtcNow).ToUnixTimeMilliseconds();
}
<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">
    <title>@(ViewData["Title"] ?? "Arena — CryptoSmith X")</title>

    @* Both faces the table cannot be read without. Self-hosted, so these are same-origin — but a
       font preload still needs crossorigin, because fonts are fetched in CORS mode whatever their
       origin, and a preload whose mode does not match the real request is a second download. *@
    <link rel="preload" href="~/ds/fonts/dm-mono-400-latin.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>
    <link rel="preload" href="~/ds/fonts/anton-400-latin.woff2" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin>

    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/ds/styles.css?v=1">
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="~/arena.css?v=4">

    @* The night register, chosen before the first paint so the page never flashes the day one at a
       reader who asked for dark. Inline and tiny on purpose: a separate file would be a round trip
       in front of the paint it exists to get right.

       With scripts off this does not run and the page renders in the day register, which is the
       system's own :root default and completely correct — the reason the INK button below starts
       hidden and this script reveals it. A control that does nothing is a small lie, and this page
       does not get to tell small ones.

       localStorage is written only when the visitor presses the button. Nothing is stored for a
       reader who never asks for anything. *@
    <script>
        (function () {
            try {
                var stored = localStorage.getItem('csx-arena-theme');
                var night = stored ? stored === 'night'
                    : window.matchMedia('(prefers-color-scheme: dark)').matches;
                if (night) { document.documentElement.setAttribute('data-theme', 'night'); }
            } catch (e) { /* Private mode refuses storage. The day register is a fine answer. */ }
        })();
    </script>
    @await RenderSectionAsync("head", required: false)
</head>
<body>
@*
    The fade's three constants travel from the server rather than being written a second time in
    JavaScript. They are the design system's rule 2, they are already in Freshness.cs, and a copy of
    them in a file nothing in CI compiles is exactly the shape of the bug this project has already
    had once: a threshold written as a literal in one place, disagreed with in another, and Kraken
    reading as degraded at 39 seconds. The amplitude is not sent at all — it is 1 − floor, derived
    on both sides, never a fourth number to keep in agreement with a third.
*@
<div class="a-sheet"
     data-now="@serverNowMs"
     data-fade-floor="@Freshness.Floor.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
     data-fade-exponent="@Freshness.Exponent.ToString(System.Globalization.CultureInfo.InvariantCulture)"
     data-degraded-windows="@Freshness.DegradedWindows">
    <i class="a-topline"></i>

    <header class="a-head">
        <span class="a-wordmark">CryptoSmith X</span>
        @if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(eyebrow))
        {
            <span class="csx-eyebrow">@eyebrow</span>
        }
        <span class="a-head-right">
            @* Both marks, in a partial because the live stream re-sends them: see _Stamps.cshtml. *@
            <partial name="_Stamps" />
            <button type="button" class="a-btn" id="a-ink" hidden>Ink</button>
        </span>
    </header>

    @RenderBody()

    <footer class="a-foot">
        @*
            "How to read this page", when the page has one. It renders HERE rather than at the end
            of the body it explains, because that is what the link under the table promises: a
            reader who clicks "How to read this page ↓" is asking to leave the table, and landing
            them in the middle of the candles would answer a different question.

            A section rather than a partial: the glossary is the pair page's own rules — a board of
            pairs has no venue cell, no crossed book and no funding interval to explain — and a
            partial rendered unconditionally would put a table's legend under a page with no table.
            required:false, so the pages that have nothing to explain print nothing.
        *@
        @await RenderSectionAsync("reading", required: false)

        <div class="a-foot-legal">
            @* Verbatim. The content rules give this line word for word and it is not paraphrased. *@
            <span class="csx-eyebrow">Not investment advice · Own funds only</span>
            <span class="csx-eyebrow a-foot-right">blynai.eu · MB „BlynAI“</span>
        </div>
    </footer>
</div>

@* Manual cache-bust rather than asp-append-version: the Script TagHelper trips a Razor compiler
   bug on this SDK and the WebApp's own pair page loads its chart the same way. Bump v= on change. *@
<script src="~/arena-ages.js?v=3" defer></script>
@await RenderSectionAsync("scripts", required: false)
</body>
</html>
